{"subjectId":"c","id":"preprocessor","title":"Preprocessor & Macros","group":"Files, Preprocessor & Program Organisation","order":49,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Before compilation, the preprocessor rewrites the source text: it pastes in headers, expands macros and keeps or drops code with conditionals. It knows nothing about C types or scope.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Directive</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>#include &lt;x.h&gt;</code> / <code>\"x.h\"</code></td><td>Paste a file (angle brackets: system paths; quotes: current dir first)</td></tr><tr><td><code>#define NAME value</code></td><td>Object-like macro</td></tr><tr><td><code>#define F(a, b) (…)</code></td><td>Function-like macro</td></tr><tr><td><code>#undef NAME</code></td><td>Remove a definition</td></tr><tr><td><code>#if</code> / <code>#elif</code> / <code>#else</code> / <code>#endif</code></td><td>Conditional compilation on constant expressions</td></tr><tr><td><code>#ifdef</code> / <code>#ifndef</code> / <code>defined(X)</code></td><td>Is a macro defined?</td></tr><tr><td><code>#error \"msg\"</code></td><td>Stop compilation with a message</td></tr><tr><td><code>#pragma</code></td><td>Implementation-specific (<code>#pragma once</code>, <code>#pragma pack</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>#line</code></td><td>Change reported line numbers (used by code generators)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Macros and their pitfalls</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\n#define SQUARE_BAD(x) x * x\n#define SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x))            /* parenthesise every argument AND the whole body */\n#define MAX(a, b) ((a) &gt; (b) ? (a) : (b))\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", SQUARE_BAD(2 + 3));   /* 2 + 3 * 2 + 3 = 11 */\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", SQUARE(2 + 3));       /* 25 */\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", 100 / SQUARE(5));     /* 4 — without outer parens: 100 / 5 * 5 = 100 */\n\n    int i = 5, j = 3;\n    int m = MAX(i++, j);                 /* i++ is evaluated TWICE */\n    printf(\"m = %d, i = %d\\n\", m, i);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>11\n25\n4\nm = 6, i = 7</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Function-like macros evaluate arguments as many times as they appear. Never pass expressions with side effects (<code>i++</code>, function calls). Prefer <code>static inline</code> functions — they are type-checked and evaluate arguments once.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i>Multi-statement macros: do { } while (0)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\n#define SWAP(a, b) do { int tmp_ = (a); (a) = (b); (b) = tmp_; } while (0)\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 1, y = 2;\n    if (x &lt; y)\n        SWAP(x, y);        /* expands to ONE statement, so the else still pairs correctly */\n    else\n        printf(\"no swap\\n\");\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2 1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-text-plus\"></i>Stringising #, token pasting ##, predefined macros</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\n#define STR(x) #x\n#define XSTR(x) STR(x)                    /* expand first, then stringise */\n#define VERSION 3\n#define DEBUG_PRINT(var) printf(#var \" = %d\\n\", var)\n#define MAKE_GETTER(field) int get_##field(void) { return field; }\n\nint width = 80;\nMAKE_GETTER(width)                        /* defines int get_width(void) */\n\nint main(void) {\n    int count = 42;\n    DEBUG_PRINT(count);\n    printf(\"%s %s\\n\", STR(VERSION), XSTR(VERSION));\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", get_width());\n    printf(\"line %d of %s, C standard %ld\\n\", __LINE__, __func__, __STDC_VERSION__);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>count = 42\nVERSION 3\n80\nline 17 of main, C standard 201710</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Macro</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>__FILE__</code>, <code>__LINE__</code></td><td>Current file name and line</td></tr><tr><td><code>__func__</code></td><td>Current function name (a predefined identifier, not a macro)</td></tr><tr><td><code>__DATE__</code>, <code>__TIME__</code></td><td>Compilation date and time</td></tr><tr><td><code>__STDC_VERSION__</code></td><td><code>199901L</code> (C99), <code>201112L</code> (C11), <code>201710L</code> (C17), <code>202311L</code> (C23)</td></tr><tr><td><code>__GNUC__</code>, <code>__clang__</code>, <code>_WIN32</code>, <code>__linux__</code>, <code>__APPLE__</code></td><td>Compiler / platform detection</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-branch\"></i>Conditional compilation</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — compiled on macOS with -DBUFFER_SIZE=1024</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\n#define LOG_LEVEL 2\n\n#ifndef BUFFER_SIZE\n#define BUFFER_SIZE 256                   /* default unless -DBUFFER_SIZE=... is passed */\n#endif\n\n#if LOG_LEVEL &gt;= 2\n#define LOG(msg) printf(\"[debug] %s\\n\", msg)\n#else\n#define LOG(msg) ((void)0)\n#endif\n\nint main(void) {\n    LOG(\"starting\");\n#if defined(__APPLE__)\n    printf(\"macOS build, buffer %d\\n\", BUFFER_SIZE);\n#elif defined(__linux__)\n    printf(\"Linux build, buffer %d\\n\", BUFFER_SIZE);\n#else\n    printf(\"other build, buffer %d\\n\", BUFFER_SIZE);\n#endif\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[debug] starting\nmacOS build, buffer 1024</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — assert aborts the program when the condition is false</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;assert.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int balance = 10;\n    assert(balance &gt;= 0);           /* active unless NDEBUG is defined */\n    balance -= 25;\n    assert(balance &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; \"balance went negative\");\n    printf(\"unreachable\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>stderr</figcaption><pre><code>Assertion failed: (balance &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; \"balance went negative\"), function main, file main.c, line 8.</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between a macro and an inline function?</summary><div><p>A macro is textual substitution before compilation: no type checking, arguments may be evaluated several times, no scope, hard to debug. An <code>inline</code> function is a real function the compiler may expand in place: arguments are evaluated once and type-checked, and it follows normal scope rules. Macros are still needed for stringising, token pasting, conditional compilation and type-generic tricks.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why wrap a multi-statement macro in do { ... } while (0)?</summary><div><p>It turns the macro into a single statement that requires a trailing semicolon, so it works correctly as the body of an unbraced <code>if</code>/<code>else</code>. A bare <code>{ … }</code> block followed by <code>;</code> would break an <code>if … else</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does #include &lt;file&gt; vs #include \"file\" do?</summary><div><p>Both paste the file’s contents. <code>\"file\"</code> searches the directory of the current file first, then the system paths; <code>&lt;file&gt;</code> searches only the system/<code>-I</code> paths. Use quotes for your own headers.</p></div></details></section>"}